# ===========================================================================
# CardDemo :: Java migration module :: Docker build-context exclusions
# ===========================================================================
#
# SCOPE AND CONTEXT ROOT
# ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
# The companion Dockerfile sits beside this file, so the image is built as
#
#     cd carddemo-java && docker build -t carddemo-java:1.0.0 .
#
# which makes THIS DIRECTORY the build context root - not the repository root.
# Everything above this directory is consequently already outside the context
# and needs no pattern whatsoever: the legacy mainframe estate under app/, the
# documentation set under docs/, the image and drawio assets under diagrams/,
# the vendor runtime archives under samples/, and the repository .git tree.
#
# Docker cannot escape the context root, so a parent-directory pattern is
# meaningless and no such pattern appears anywhere in this file. Wanting one is
# a symptom of invoking the build from the wrong directory.
#
#
# PATTERN SEMANTICS - READ THIS BEFORE EDITING
# ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
# .dockerignore is NOT .gitignore. The behavioural difference that matters
# here was verified against this Docker engine rather than assumed:
#
#     *.pem       matches secret.pem AT THE CONTEXT ROOT ONLY.
#                 It does NOT match src/test/resources/secret.pem.
#     **/*.pem    matches secret.pem at ANY depth, root included.
#     name/       prunes that directory and its entire subtree.
#     !pattern    re-includes a previously excluded path; LAST match wins.
#
# Every extension rule below is therefore written twice - once bare for the
# context root and once with a "**/" prefix for every deeper level. Dropping
# the "**/" form silently reopens the hole it was written to close, which for
# the credential rules in section 3 is a security regression, not merely a
# build-context size regression.
#
#
# THE OPPOSITE FAILURE MODE - EXCLUDING TOO MUCH
# ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
# A .dockerignore fails in two directions. Excluding too little bloats the
# context, destroys layer caching, and risks a stale host-compiled class file
# reaching the image instead of one the builder stage compiled under
# -Xlint:all -Werror. Excluding too much silently removes a load-bearing input
# and either breaks the build or, worse, produces an image that fails at
# startup. Two concrete traps in THIS module, both verified by experiment:
#
#   1. "config/" below is ROOT-ANCHORED ON PURPOSE. It excludes the host-side
#      Prometheus and Grafana tree at ./config/. It must NEVER be promoted to
#      "**/config/", because that also matches src/main/java/com/carddemo/config
#      and would strip SecurityConfig, BatchConfig, AwsConfig, JwtTokenProvider,
#      ObservabilityConfig and MenuOptionCatalog out of the build context.
#      Root-anchored keeps the host tree out AND the Java package in.
#
#   2. No blanket extension rule may be added for the extensions that carry
#      real application resources: .yml, .yaml, .xml, .properties, .sql, .json
#      and .txt. A single careless "*.properties" removes the Maven Wrapper
#      descriptor; a careless "*.yml" removes the Spring profile files; a
#      careless "*.txt" removes banner.txt and the fixed-width test fixtures.
#      The credential rules in section 3 are written narrowly for exactly this
#      reason and are proven in section 6.
#
#
# CASING
# ~~~~~~
# Patterns are matched case-sensitively and the wider CardDemo estate mixes
# extension casing, so every extension rule is written in both cases. This
# mirrors the discipline of the .gitignore beside this file.
#
#
# WHAT MUST REACH THE BUILDER STAGE - NEVER EXCLUDE THESE
# ~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
# The multi-stage Dockerfile copies exactly three things, in this order:
#
#     COPY .mvn/ .mvn/        ->  .mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.properties
#     COPY mvnw pom.xml ./    ->  mvnw, pom.xml
#     COPY src/ src/          ->  the whole source tree
#
# pom.xml, mvnw, .mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.properties and src/ are therefore
# load-bearing: excluding any one of them does not degrade the build, it breaks
# it. Section 6 restates that as explicit re-inclusions so the guarantee
# survives a careless future edit to the sections above.
#
# src/test/ IS DELIBERATELY KEPT IN THE CONTEXT. The builder stage runs
# "./mvnw -B -ntp clean package -DskipTests", which skips test EXECUTION but
# still runs the test-compile phase, and it copies the whole src/ tree in a
# single COPY. Excluding src/test/ would leave this file and the Dockerfile
# contradicting each other. Test EXECUTION belongs to CI, which runs
# "./mvnw -B clean verify" against the same sources. The fixed-width golden
# fixtures under src/test/resources/fixtures/ stay in the repository and in the
# context for that reason.
#
# ===========================================================================


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. MAVEN BUILD OUTPUT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The single highest-value group in this file. Without it a developer's local
# target/ - compiled classes, the packaged jar, JaCoCo .exec data, surefire
# and failsafe reports - is uploaded on every docker build. That defeats layer
# caching and risks a stale host-compiled class reaching the image instead of
# one the builder stage compiled itself. The image must contain exactly what
# the builder compiled, nothing the host happened to leave lying around.
target/
**/target/

# Alternative output directory names, root-anchored: no directory of these
# names exists anywhere under src/, and anchoring keeps it that way.
build/
out/
bin/
dist/

# Stray archives, class files, coverage data and heap dumps at any depth.
*.jar
*.JAR
**/*.jar
**/*.JAR
*.war
*.WAR
**/*.war
**/*.WAR
*.ear
*.EAR
**/*.ear
**/*.EAR
*.class
*.CLASS
**/*.class
**/*.CLASS
*.exec
*.EXEC
**/*.exec
**/*.EXEC
*.hprof
*.HPROF
**/*.hprof
**/*.HPROF

# Maven Wrapper: the descriptor is REQUIRED inside the builder stage and is
# re-included in section 6. Only a locally regenerated launcher jar is
# excluded - this project pins distributionType=only-script, so no jar is
# committed and none may be smuggled into an image layer.
.mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.jar
.mvn/.develocity/
.mvn/.gradle-enterprise/

# Residue left behind by Maven release, shade, flatten and buildnumber
# plugins. Each is a distinct filename, so none of them can match pom.xml.
dependency-reduced-pom.xml
.flattened-pom.xml
pom.xml.releaseBackup
pom.xml.versionsBackup
pom.xml.next
pom.xml.tag
release.properties
buildNumber.properties


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. VALIDATION GATE EVIDENCE AND REPORT OUTPUT
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated evidence artifacts. They are consumed by the repository's gate
# evidence document; none of them belongs inside a runtime image.

# OWASP dependency-check. The scanner's vulnerability database is configured
# outside this module, but a plugin default or a local override can drop it
# here, and it is hundreds of megabytes of unscanned third-party data. Keeping
# it out of the context is both a size and a supply-chain hygiene measure.
dependency-check-report.*
**/dependency-check-report.*
.dependency-check/
**/.dependency-check/
dependency-check-data/
**/dependency-check-data/

# Surefire, failsafe, JaCoCo and the Maven site report tree. The exec files
# and reports normally live under target/ and are already pruned above; these
# rules cover a relocated reportsDirectory or destFile configuration.
surefire-reports/
**/surefire-reports/
failsafe-reports/
**/failsafe-reports/
jacoco*.exec
**/jacoco*.exec
site/
**/site/
test-output/
**/test-output/


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. CREDENTIALS AND HOST-ONLY SECRET MATERIAL
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Defence in depth for the binding requirement that no credential is hardcoded
# and every secret is resolved from an environment variable or a vault
# reference with no fallback default.
#
# The reason this section exists even though nothing here is committed: any
# file copied into an image layer is PERMANENTLY RECOVERABLE FROM THAT LAYER,
# even when a later layer deletes it. A leaked credential cannot be un-shipped
# by an rm in a subsequent RUN. Excluding it from the context is the only
# reliable control, and it must hold at every depth - hence the "**/" twins.

# Environment files. Never baked into an image; supplied at run time instead.
# No re-inclusion is granted for a ".env.example" template. Such a file is a
# developer aid that belongs in version control, not in an image: the Dockerfile
# copies no environment file, docker-compose declares no env_file, and the
# production profile resolves every secret from a real environment variable with
# no fallback. Excluding the whole family unconditionally is therefore both safe
# and stricter, and it removes any chance of a future edit turning a template
# into a file that carries real values.
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*

# Keys, certificates and keystores.
*.pem
*.PEM
**/*.pem
**/*.PEM
*.key
*.KEY
**/*.key
**/*.KEY
*.crt
*.CRT
**/*.crt
**/*.CRT
*.cer
*.CER
**/*.cer
**/*.CER
*.p12
*.P12
**/*.p12
**/*.P12
*.pfx
*.PFX
**/*.pfx
**/*.PFX
*.jks
*.JKS
**/*.jks
**/*.JKS
*.keystore
*.KEYSTORE
**/*.keystore
**/*.KEYSTORE
*.truststore
*.TRUSTSTORE
**/*.truststore
**/*.TRUSTSTORE
id_rsa
id_rsa.*
id_ed25519
id_ed25519.*
**/id_rsa
**/id_rsa.*
**/id_ed25519
**/id_ed25519.*
secrets/
**/secrets/

# Cloud credential material. The AWS surface in scope is S3, SQS and SNS, all
# emulated locally by LocalStack Community; no long-lived credential belongs
# anywhere near the build context.
credentials
credentials.*
**/credentials
**/credentials.*
aws.properties
**/aws.properties
.aws/
**/.aws/

# Local, uncommitted configuration overlays. These rules are deliberately
# NARROW so that the committed Spring profile descriptors are never matched:
#   application.yml, application-local.yml and application-prod.yml under
#   src/main/resources, and application-test.yml under src/test/resources,
#   are required deliverables and are re-included explicitly in section 6.
# application-prod.yml is safe to ship precisely because it contains no secret
# value - only unresolved environment-variable references with no fallback, so
# a missing secret fails startup instead of silently binding a default value.
application-secret*.yml
application-secret*.yaml
**/application-secret*.yml
**/application-secret*.yaml
application-*.local.yml
application-*.local.yaml
**/application-*.local.yml
**/application-*.local.yaml
application-local.properties
**/application-local.properties


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. HOST-SIDE ORCHESTRATION, OBSERVABILITY AND DOCUMENTATION
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Everything in this section is used BY the host or bind-mounted INTO a
# sibling container by Compose. None of it is content of the application image.

# Compose orchestrates the container; it is not part of it. Excluding the
# Dockerfile and this file is supported and intentional - the builder still
# receives both out of band, and excluding them removes any possibility of
# COPYing build metadata into the shipped image.
docker-compose.yml
docker-compose.yaml
docker-compose.*.yml
docker-compose.*.yaml
compose.yml
compose.yaml
Dockerfile*
.dockerignore

# Observability configuration: the Prometheus scrape definition and the
# Grafana datasource, dashboard-provider and dashboard files. Compose
# bind-mounts these into the Prometheus and Grafana services; the application
# image never reads them.
#
# ROOT-ANCHORED ON PURPOSE - see "THE OPPOSITE FAILURE MODE" in the header.
# Promoting this to "**/config/" would also match
# src/main/java/com/carddemo/config and break the build.
config/

# LocalStack bootstrap. The init hook that creates the S3 staging bucket, the
# SQS FIFO job queue and the SNS notification topic is mounted into the
# LocalStack container, not into this image. Root-anchored for the same reason
# as config/ above.
localstack/

# Compose bind-mount data directories. These are the largest single risk to
# context size in normal local use: a PostgreSQL data directory alone can
# reach gigabytes, and none of it is remotely image content.
postgres-data/
**/postgres-data/
localstack-data/
**/localstack-data/
prometheus-data/
**/prometheus-data/
grafana-data/
**/grafana-data/
volume/
volumes/
**/volume/
**/volumes/
.localstack/
**/.localstack/

# The Windows Maven Wrapper launcher. The builder stage copies "mvnw pom.xml"
# only, and a Windows batch launcher has no role inside a Linux image. NOTE:
# if the Dockerfile's COPY is ever widened to name mvnw.cmd explicitly, this
# line must be deleted or the build will fail. The POSIX launcher mvnw is NOT
# excluded and is re-included explicitly in section 6.
mvnw.cmd

# Documentation. Excluding it keeps the context minimal and stops a prose edit
# from invalidating every downstream image layer. No Markdown file is a build
# input, and none exists anywhere under src/.
*.md
*.MD
**/*.md
**/*.MD
*.adoc
**/*.adoc

# LICENSE and NOTICE are deliberately NOT excluded. They live one level above
# this context root today, but if a module-scoped copy is ever added it should
# ship with the image: license continuity is a requirement of this migration,
# and container compliance scanners look for exactly those two filenames.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. VERSION CONTROL, IDE, OS AND JVM CRASH ARTIFACTS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Defensive: the repository .git tree lives one level above this context root
# and is already outside it. The rules are kept anyway because a git worktree,
# a submodule checkout or a nested clone can put a .git directory inside the
# module, and a .git tree in an image layer ships the FULL repository history
# - including any credential that was ever committed and later removed.
.git
.git/
**/.git
**/.git/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.gitmodules
.github/

# .gitkeep is deliberately NOT excluded. The empty marker files under
# src/main/resources/db/seed and src/main/resources/lookup are what make those
# directories exist on disk, and a classpath Flyway or resource location that
# resolves to an absent directory fails at startup. A zero-byte marker file
# costs nothing; a missing directory costs a boot failure. Verified: all three
# marker files are present in the shipped jar, and db/seed exists only because
# of its own.

# JetBrains, Eclipse, VS Code and NetBeans metadata.
.idea/
*.iml
*.IML
**/*.iml
**/*.IML
*.ipr
*.IPR
*.iws
*.IWS
.classpath
.project
.settings/
.factorypath
.apt_generated/
.apt_generated_tests/
.vscode/
nbproject/
nbbuild/
nbdist/
.nb-gradle/

# Editor scratch files, patch residue and OS metadata.
*~
**/*~
*.swp
*.SWP
**/*.swp
**/*.SWP
*.swo
*.SWO
**/*.swo
**/*.SWO
*.bak
*.BAK
**/*.bak
**/*.BAK
*.orig
*.ORIG
**/*.orig
**/*.ORIG
*.rej
*.REJ
**/*.rej
**/*.REJ
*.tmp
**/*.tmp
.DS_Store
**/.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
Desktop.ini
desktop.ini

# Application logs and JVM failure artifacts. Structured logging goes to the
# container's stdout, so no log file is ever an image input.
*.log
*.LOG
*.log.*
**/*.log
**/*.LOG
**/*.log.*
logs/
**/logs/
hs_err_pid*
**/hs_err_pid*
replay_pid*
**/replay_pid*
heapdump*
**/heapdump*
core
core.[0-9]*
**/core.[0-9]*

# Temporary artifacts produced by automated validation runs. Never committed,
# never shipped.
blitzy_adhoc_test_*
**/blitzy_adhoc_test_*


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. EXPLICIT RE-INCLUSIONS - THE BUILD-INPUT GUARANTEE
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing above matches any of the paths below; these lines are a deliberate,
# self-documenting safety net. Because the LAST matching rule wins, they
# guarantee that a future blanket rule added to sections 1 to 5 - "*.xml",
# "*.properties", "*.yml", "*.txt", "*.json" or "*.sql" - cannot silently
# remove a load-bearing build input.
#
# These are intentionally FILE-PRECISE. A broad "!src/**" is NOT used, because
# it would undo the "**/*.class" and "**/*.jar" rules in section 1 for any
# stale artifact sitting inside the source tree.

# The three inputs the builder stage copies before the source tree.
!pom.xml
!mvnw
!.mvn/wrapper/maven-wrapper.properties

# Spring configuration that must be packaged into the jar.
!src/main/resources/application.yml
!src/main/resources/application-local.yml
!src/main/resources/application-test.yml
!src/main/resources/application-prod.yml
!src/main/resources/logback-spring.xml
!src/main/resources/banner.txt

# Flyway migrations and the externalized validation lookup tables.
!src/main/resources/db/migration/**
!src/main/resources/db/seed/**
!src/main/resources/lookup/**

# The test profile and the fixed-width golden fixtures that the byte-equality
# assertions compare against. Kept in the context because the builder copies
# the whole src/ tree; see the src/test/ note in the header.
!src/test/resources/application-test.yml
!src/test/resources/fixtures/input/**
!src/test/resources/fixtures/expected/**
